WEBVTT

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:01.254
Four objects.

00:00:01.294 --> 00:00:03.480
So why does
furniture take ‘is’?

00:00:04.003 --> 00:00:06.861
A chair, a table,
a sofa and a bed.

00:00:06.901 --> 00:00:08.071
I can count them:

00:00:08.111 --> 00:00:10.024
one chair,
two chairs.

00:00:10.064 --> 00:00:11.527
But furniture
is the overall label

00:00:11.567 --> 00:00:12.754
for the whole
collection,

00:00:12.794 --> 00:00:14.721
not the name
of each item.

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:17.719
English treats that
word as uncountable.

00:00:17.759 --> 00:00:19.560
So: ‘The furniture
is expensive.’

00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:20.756
Not ‘are’.

00:00:21.703 --> 00:00:22.638
Need a number?

00:00:22.678 --> 00:00:23.816
Count pieces:

00:00:23.856 --> 00:00:26.507
‘Two pieces of furniture
are expensive.’

00:00:26.547 --> 00:00:27.739
The number
goes on pieces,

00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:29.469
so the verb
is plural.

00:00:30.403 --> 00:00:32.256
Same picture
with luggage:

00:00:32.296 --> 00:00:33.460
‘The luggage
is heavy’,

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.672
but ‘three pieces
of luggage are heavy’.

00:00:35.712 --> 00:00:36.771
See what
the noun names,

00:00:36.811 --> 00:00:38.385
then choose
the verb.

